WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
It's a new day on Tesla Short's Time Daily, episode 453 for April 28, 2026.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.100
I'm Patrick coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.060
Let's get into what's moving in the Tesla world today.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.840
Tesla is hiring engineers to build a wireless battery management system for

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.660
CyberCab that removes heavy wiring harnesses across its vehicles.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.180
Tesla is actively recruiting a staff electrical design engineer to develop

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.920
wireless battery management systems specifically for the CyberCab and

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.660
next generation battery electronics.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.020
The goal is to replace heavy copper wiring harnesses with small radio

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.820
frequency chips on each module.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.800
This simplifies automated assembly because robots would no longer need to

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.000
route and connect complex wire bundles.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.160
It cuts weight, which improves efficiency and range,

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.180
while also reducing vibration-related failure points over hundreds of

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.160
thousands of kilometers.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.560
Recycling becomes easier too since there is less copper to separate at

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.100
end of life.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.100
The plan is to spread this approach across everything Tesla builds which

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.900
represents a meaningful shift in how battery packs are engineered from the start.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.980
What stands out to me is how this targets manufacturing friction at its source

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.340
rather than just tweaking existing designs.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.900
This one actually lines up perfectly with the deeper first

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.620
principles piece we are doing this week, so let us jump straight into that.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.400
Most people still imagine battery packs as complicated

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.760
labor-intensive bundles of copper wiring.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.200
But the physics shows those harnesses are mostly dead weight and

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.940
manufacturing friction once you have reliable wireless data transfer.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.500
Removing them frees up space for more cells and cuts mass that directly hurts range.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.160
It also eliminates vibration-failure points that accumulate over long distances

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.320
and thermal cycles.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.460
This lets robots simply drop modules in place during assembly

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.240
which could dramatically change production speed and cost.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.360
If it delivers even half the expected gains,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.140
this quiet change could shift the cost structure enough

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.680
to make lower price electric vehicles truly viable at volume.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.700
It becomes a foundational move that compounds across cars, energy storage, and robots.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:04.660
Tesla tends to attack problems by controlling the full stack and iterating with real fleet data.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.180
Starting with the cybercab and next-generation vehicles gives them a clean sheet

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.780
to prove the architecture first.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.960
They have taken similar approaches before with structural battery packs

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.800
accepting early challenges for long-term advantage.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.220
